1 OCCU Educadora
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Maestra, Vicedirectora del Colegio Santa Rosa y Directora de la Escuela Central de señoritas de San Juan (Argentina). El gobierno no le concedió jubilación porque nunca había cobrado sueldo por sus tareas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in San Juan 30 October 1804, daughter of Paula Albarracín and Clemente Sarmiento, and sister of D.F. Sarmiento. She participated in his theatrical tertulias that he held during his first exile. On 9 July 1839 Sarmiento made h
2 CONC er vice-director and teacher at the Colegio de Pensionistas, Santa Rosa. She was there for three years. On 1 January 1842 the family was no longer able to stay in the country and she crossed the Andes with her mother and sisters. 
2 CONC They settled in San Felipe de Aconagua and organised, with her sister, Procesa, a college San Juan y Santa María, known by the Chileans as el Colegio de las Sarmiento. She returned to San Juan in 1848, but later returned to Chile.
2 CONC  In 1852 they returned to San Juan where she opened a private school. Her brother was governor of San Juan in 1862 and she took charge of the newly founded Escuela Colegio Central de las Señoritas. In 1865 she was one of the found
2 CONC ers of the Sociedad de Beneficencia and was elected president in 1867 and in 1881. She opened another school in 1868. She helped those suffering during the yellow fever epidemic in Buenos Aires. She painted and drew and was partic
2 CONC ularly skilled at embroidery. After her brother's death she wrote Algunos rasgos de la vida de Domingo Faustino Sarmiento. She died on 21 March 1900. (Sosa de Newton, 585-586)
2 CONT
2 CONT Life Events
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 1804 She was born in San Juan 30 October 1804. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Other 1839 On 9 July 1839 she became director of the Colegio de Pensionistas, Santa Rosa. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Other 1842 On 1 January 1842 she and her family emigrated to San Felipe Aconcagua. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Other 1848 She briefly returned to San Juan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Other 1852 She opened a private school in San Juan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Other 1862 She took charge of the Escuela Colegio Central de las Señoritas, San Juan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Other 1865 She co-founded the Sociedad de Beneficencia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Other 1867 She was elected president of the Sociedad de Beneficencia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Other 1881 She was elected president of the Sociedad de Beneficencia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 1900 She died on 21 March 1900. 
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Sosa de Newton, Lily, (1986), Diccionario biográfico de mujeres argentinas
