1 OCCU profesora
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Born in San Felipe Aconagua in July 1831, she was the daughter of Domingo Faustino Sarmiento. Her mother died when she was young and her father took her to San Juan when she was 5 to be cared for by his mother and 
2 CONC sisters. She was educated in the Colegio de Santa Rosa, founded by her father. She fled to San Felipe Aconagua with her grandmother and aunts in 1842 where she continued her studies. Her father travelled in Europe and the United S
2 CONC tates bringing back with him a young Frenchman, Julio Belin, who he set up in a printing business. Within a year he married Faustina Sarmiento (1848). They had 6 children. They moved back to San Juan after Rosas's fall. She was wi
2 CONC dowed in 1865 and devoted herself to working with her aunt Bienvenida. She participated in the Sociedad de Madres Cristianas, a precursor to the Sociedad de Beneficencia. She helped during the yellow fever epidemic in Buenos Aires
2 CONC . On 6 March 1866 she became a teacher in the Escuela Modelo, of which Bienvenida was directora. She became directora of the Escuela Superior de Niñas in 1873. She was awarded a pension of 300 pesos per month after her father died
2 CONC  in 1888. She died in Buenos Aires on 4 December 1904. (Sosa de Newton, 586-587)
2 CONT
2 CONT Life Events
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 1831 She was born in San Felipe Aconagua in July 1831. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Other 1836 Her father brought her to San Juan around this time. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Other 1842 She fled to San Felipe Aconagua with her grandmother and aunts. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Married 1848 She married Julio Belin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Other 1865 She was widowed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Other 1866 On 6 March 1866 she became a teacher in the Escuela Modelo. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Other 1873 She became directora of the Escuela Superior de Niñas. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Other 1888 She was awarded a pension of 300 pesos per month. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 1904 She died in Buenos Aires on 4 December 1904. 
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Sosa de Newton, Lily, (1986), Diccionario biográfico de mujeres argentinas
